ilitation outcomes by facilitating higher training intensity and enabling task-oriented exercises.\n\n  * Counterexamples to the Balogh-Linz-Patkós Conjecture\n\nJia-Bao Yang, Leilei Zhang\n\nJul 07 2026 math.CO arXiv:2607.03026v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2607.03026, author = {Jia-Bao Yang and Leilei Zhang}, title = {{C}ounterexamples to the {B}alogh-{L}inz-{P}atkós {C}onjecture}, year = {2026}, eprint = {2607.03026}, note = {arXiv:2607.03026v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nA set system $\\mathcal{F}$ is called $t$-intersecting if $|A\\cap B|\\ge t$ for every pair of sets $A,B\\in \\mathcal{F}.$ A set system $\\mathcal{F}$ is $k$-Sperner if it does not contain a chain of length $k+1$. Balogh, Linz and Patkós (Combinatorial Theory, 2023) conjectured an extremal result for $t$-intersecting $k$-Sperner families when $n+t$ is odd. In this note we give an explicit construction that is $t$-intersecting and $k$-Sperner, and whose size exceeds that of the conjectured fixed-star construction for infinitely many values of $n$. Consequently, we disprove the Balogh-Linz-Patkós conjecture for all $t\\ge 2$ and $k\\ge 2$ satisfying $k(t-1)\\ge t+1$.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTop arXiv papers (https://scirate.com/?date=2026-07-14&page=36&range=1)\nciteturn2search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 months ago; Crawled: 2 days ago;   * Steklov Spectral Geometry for Annular Surfaces: Inverse spectral results and isospectral compactness ... Jul 14 2026 math.SP arXiv:2607.10108v1 ... @misc{2607.10108, author = {Yujun Jin and Zuoqin Wang}, title = {{S}teklov {S}pectral {G}eometry for {A}nnular {S}urfaces: {I}nverse spectral results and isospectral compactness}, year = {2026}, eprint = {2607.10108}, note = {arXiv:2607.10108v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\n  * Hypergraph Erdős--Rogers functions with consecutive clique sizes\n\nQizhong Lin, Lin Niu\n\nJul 14 2026 math.CO arXiv:2607.10111v2\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2607.10111, author = {Qizhong Lin and Lin Niu}, title = {{H}ypergraph {E}rdős--{R}ogers functions with consecutive clique sizes}, year = {2026}, eprint = {2607.10111}, note = {arXiv:2607.10111v2} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nFor integers \\(k\\le s<t\\), the hypergraph Erdős--Rogers function \\(f^(k)_s,t(n)\\)is the largest integer \\(m\\)such that every \\(n\\)-vertex \\(K_t^(k)\\)-free \\(k\\)-graph contains a set of \\(m\\)vertices spanning no copy of \\(K_s^(k)\\). We prove that, for every fixed \\(s\\ge4\\), \\[f^(4)_s,s+1(n)=(\\log n)^o(1), \\]thereby resolving a problem posed by Conlon, Fox and Sudakov. The key input is a new \\(3\\)-uniform estimate: for every fixed \\(s\\ge3\\), \\(f^(3)_s,s+1(n)=O(\\frac\\log n\\log\\log n)\\), which improves the logarithmic upper bound of Dudek and Mubayi. \nYujun Jin, Zuoqin Wang\n\nJul 14 2026 math.SP arXiv:2607.10108v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2607.10108, author = {Yujun Jin and Zuoqin Wang}, title = {{S}teklov {S}pectral {G}eometry for {A}nnular {S}urfaces: {I}nverse spectral results and isospectral compactness}, year = {2026}, eprint = {2607.10108}, note = {arXiv:2607.10108v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nWe study the inverse spectral problems for the Steklov spectrum on compact surfaces with boundary. We prove that among flat annular surfaces, the lateral surface of a conical frustum is uniquely determined by its Steklov spectrum. As a consequence, each circular annulus is uniquely determined among all planar domains, providing the first example of a non-simply connected Euclidean domain with this property. Furthermore, we show that any family of Steklov isospectral flat annular surfaces is compact in the $C^\\infty$ topology, extending previous results for simply connected planar domains. These results are established through a detailed analysis of the spectral zeta function and the zeta-regularized determinant associated with the Dirichlet-to-Neumann operator for annular surfaces.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSteklov Spectral Geometry for Annular Surfaces: Inverse spectral results and isospectral compactness (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/409443954_Steklov_Spectral_Geometry_for_Annular_Surfaces_Inverse_spectral_results_and_isospectral_compactness)\nciteturn2search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; # Steklov Spectral Geometry for Annular Surfaces: Inverse spectral results and isospectral compactness ... DOI:10.48550/arXiv.2607.10108\n\n# Steklov Spectral Geometry for Annular Surfaces: Inverse spectral results and isospectral compactness\n\n  * July 2026\n\nDOI:10.48550/arXiv.2607.10108\n\nAuthors:\n\nYujun Jin\n\nYujun Jin\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nZuoqin Wang\n\nZuoqin Wang\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe study the inverse spectral problems for the Steklov spectrum on compact surfaces with boundary. We prove that among flat annular surfaces, the lateral surface of a conical frustum is uniquely determined by its Steklov spectrum. As a consequence, each circular annulus is uniquely determined among all planar domains, providing the first example of a non-simply connected Euclidean domain with this property. Furthermore, we show that any family of Steklov isospectral flat annular surfaces is compact in the $C^\\infty$ topology, extending previous results for simply connected planar domains. These results are established through a detailed analysis of the spectral zeta function and the zeta-regularized determinant associated with the Dirichlet-to-Neumann operator for annular surfaces.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2606.12194] Beating Product Constructions for Linear Equations Over Finite Fields - Academus scientific article reader (https://academ.us/article/2606